Right, let us start from the beginning.

In fact, let me go back even earlier and apologize, because I was misled by a non-standard feature here. As far as I can see, the current proposal would hardly facilitate fingerprinting for browsers which merely implement standard web platform APIs. However, Blink's implementation of the Cookie Store API adds extra properties, including `expires`, so that...
`cookieStore.get('foo').then(cookie=>alert(cookie.expires))`
...would display cookie `foo`'s expiry time.

If every user was to use a browser based on Blink or behaving that way, and Alice was the only one with a limit of 2000 days while everyone else used a lower limit, then servers could detect Alice simply by sending a cookie set to expire after 9999 days and detecting that Alice would send back a cookie expiring after 2000 days.

Thank you for questioning @miketaylr and @johannhof, I adjusted my above comment.

Regarding the confusion of DevTools users I mentioned in my first comment, beyond logging a message, a solution could be to store each constrained value with a flag indicating that the datetime was altered.

```js
const year = 1000 * 60 * 60 *24 * 365 ;
const lifespan = year * 9999;
const sinceEpoch = Date.now();
const expiration = sinceEpoch+lifespan;
//cookieStore.delete('foo');
await cookieStore.set(
  {
      name: 'foo',
      value: 'bar',
      expires: expiration
    });
cookieStore.get('foo').then(cookie=>{
     if (cookie.expires != expiration) {
         alert("This browser appears to alter cookie expiration times, so that cookies expire after a maximum of " + ( cookie.expires - sinceEpoch)/year + " years.\nRequested expiration: " + Math.floor(expiration/1000) + " s after epoch\nRevised expiration: " + Math.floor(cookie.expires/1000) + " s after epoch");
		}
});
```

@arichiv: 

> The fingerprinting here is to the resolution of a specific browser engine (or range of versions of a browser)?

Yes, but not only that. RFC 6265 does not prevent a user agent from letting users set a custom cap. Even if there was a single browser and that browser was based on Blink, Alice could be the only user with a limit of 2000 days. With this proposal, a site aware of that could identify Alice by her limit alone, whereas currently (with RFC 6265), Alice could use [the "randomization" strategy I described above](https://github.com/httpwg/http-extensions/issues/1600#issuecomment-3608219553) to cover her tracks.

