@guoye-zhang I wish the PATCH spec's wording matched its intent if that is the intent, because I'm not confident that it is. I wouldn't be surprised if it's also just an arbitrary semantic property of the PATCH method that is communicated to users of an HTTP application's API via that method, and that possibility makes me uncomfortable. After all, I don't believe any such requirement applies to POST, and this isn't just a recommendation but a requirement. But I will just violate the letter of the PATCH spec here, because while I'm not 100% sure atomicity isn't in the spirit of PATCH, I am 100% sure atomicity isn't in the spirit of resumable uploads. I just don't know why we don't choose an HTTP method that accurately reflects that.

> incrementally processing the request body during a resumable upload is no worse than processing a regular request body stream.

I think you're misunderstanding what I'm saying the problem is. I'm not talking about the regular processing inherent to resumable uploads. A particular application may concurrently expose a streaming download to another client from an in-progress upload (possibly an upload done in a single large PATCH request after an empty POST request), and that _is_ exposing the partially modified representation outside. Both each PATCH and the series of PATCHes would intentionally be measurably not atomic as a core feature of that application's functionality, even to a different client than the uploader.

