@jricher 

> Is the suggestion that this constraint be applied regardless of algorithm name?

Yes.

For HTTP/3, order of frame arrival isn't guaranteed.  But that's okay -- you can (and do) say that anything you prioritize must be within the set of Stream IDs the client is allowed to open.  QUIC makes sure both sides are sufficiently synchronized on the stream state.  A MUST is fine, because the sender's view of the limit is always less than or equal to the server's view of the limit.

For HTTP/2 and request streams, a MUST is fine because the server will consume changes to stream state exactly in the order they're sent over the TCP connection.  There is no overtaking possible.

@LPardue, you're right that my formulation above omits push streams.  That restriction should be scoped to talking about updating priority for client-initiated requests; the restriction for push would be any server-initiated stream that is not in the "idle" state.  (If it's closed already, the server can discard the frame without processing; otherwise, the number is constrained by the actual number of promises the server has sent and not fulfilled, which is entirely in its control.)

